---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/scenes/Hero.astro';
import WhyNow from '../components/scenes/WhyNow.astro';
import MethodScene from '../components/scenes/MethodScene.astro';
import Stake from '../components/scroll-Stake-alt.astro';
import PilotsScene from '../components/scenes/PilotsScene.astro';
import ForumScene from '../components/scenes/ForumScene.astro';
import PeopleScene from '../components/scenes/PeopleScene.astro';
---
<!-- Seven beats. No two adjacent scenes land at the same weight, on the same
     ground, or in the same composition:
       Hero      photograph · poster left, named ledger low     100svh
       WhyNow    cream · narrow left column, vertical rule, claim right
       Method    ink-700 PANEL · CENTRED — the only centred scene on the site
       Stake     photograph · ONE sentence, set RIGHT               108svh
       Pilots    ink-900 · the long ledger, flush left
       Forum     photograph · THE HELD SCENE — pinned, 2.9 viewports
       People    ink-900 head, then a full-bleed ink-800 WALL of names

     FOUR ARMATURES. The site's default move — micro label at the left rail,
     hairline ruled the full frame, block hung beneath — belongs to Hero,
     Pilots and Forum. WhyNow turns it ninety degrees (the division is
     vertical, the major column is the right one). Method abandons the left
     edge and sets on the centre line. Stake abandons the armature entirely:
     no label, no rule, no counter, no eyebrow, one sentence set right.

     THE EXCEPTION, SPENT ONCE. Stake is 108svh ON DESKTOP — taller than the
     viewport it is read in, the only element on the site that is. It exists
     because four documentary photographs were in the repo, referenced in the
     DOM, and rendered to nobody: they lived inside a 224x280px cursor plate
     that never loaded and was display:none on a phone, and the 1,059px
     Pilots ledger below held no image at all. That plate is now GONE — an
     uncredited, aria-hidden, desktop-only ornament that put identifiable
     people (one of them a child) under a moving cursor to no argument; the
     ledger's hover state is ink only, and /pilots/ shows the photographs at
     size with credits. The 108svh exception is desktop-only for the same
     class of reason: on a 390px screen the tall slot filled the whole
     viewport with two faces and pushed the sentence below the fold, so the
     phone gets a band that holds the picture and its line in one screen. One of them is now the largest object
     on the page, on every viewport. It is also the hinge between Method
     (abstract) and Pilots (concrete), which were consecutive and opened with
     the identical three-element overture.

     TONAL PACING, MEASURED ON THE SHIPPED PAGE. Wave 4 opened with "79% of
     the page inside a 5.7-point L* band". `tools/tone-meter.mjs`, run against
     the final build — real scroll positions, gutter columns, 20px rows:

         8,528px · span L* 2.5 -> 93.9 · 27 grounds, 8 of them longer than a
         third of a viewport · no single band holds more than 13% of the
         scroll · 52% of the scroll within 9 L* of the navy floor

         hero photograph     200– 780   L* 29 – 44
         cream WhyNow        920–1420   L* 93.8
         METHOD PANEL       1440–1780   L* 14.5
         Stake photograph   2880–3120   L* 24
         Pilots ledger      3140–4260   L*  3.7   <- the one flat run left
         THE HELD FORUM     4740–6220   L* 40 -> 69.5 -> 4, inside one scene
         board wall         7460–7980   L* 14.5

     The Pilots ledger is still 1,120px of one value; this file does not own
     it. The hold is what now answers it — the ledger's flat navy runs
     straight into a picture that opens out of that exact value.
     GROUND CLASSES SPENT: two — .on-cream once (WhyNow) and .on-panel twice
     (Method, the board wall). Under the ceiling in tokens.css.

     The People wall runs flush into the footer, so the page ends on a hard
     tonal edge rather than on 290px of empty navy.

     THE STAKE SHIPS AS VERSION (b). `scroll-Stake-alt.astro` is imported
     above and `scroll-Stake.astro` is not. The desktop composition — the
     one a critic named — is byte-identical; what changes is the sentence
     ("The test is ordinary life." for "For the median family.") and the
     phone crop. Both were editorial, not aesthetic: the old line printed a
     statistician's abstraction over an identifiable woman in a U.S.
     Department of Agriculture photograph, and the old phone crop centred her
     face, in focus, at full width. (b)'s own phone crop was measured again
     in round 2 and moved further — 44%/86% at zoom 1.3 — because at 46%/66%
     her full profile was still across the top third of the frame at the head
     of the scene. Screenshots of both versions: progress/shots/
     w4r2-b-shipped/ and w4r2-a-original/.

     ONE SCENE ON THIS PAGE IS PINNED, and it is the Forum. `HeldScene` may
     be spent exactly once site-wide; `[data-hold]` appears once in the whole
     of `dist/`, here. The two candidates this file used to argue over are
     still not it, for the reasons they were rejected:

       Hero    holds the four 2026 partners BY NAME inside the first screen.
               They are the only credential this institute has. Pinning the
               hero puts that ledger two and a half viewport heights below
               the fold, behind a hold, on the front door.
       Stake   is the composition a critic named, and it works because one
               sentence is ranged right into the patch of wall the crop was
               chosen to leave, at the height it is empty. HeldScene sets
               its beats in a band at the FOOT of the frame. Pinning this
               scene does not enhance that composition; it replaces it.

     This file used to send the hold to /forum/ on the reference's nomination
     (refs/MOTION-FINDINGS.md: "most likely the Forum, whose photograph is the
     most evocative asset the Institute has"). It went there and it did not
     ship: `HeldScene` existed only in `src/pages/_lab.astro`, which is
     underscore-prefixed and never built, so the site shipped ZERO held scenes
     and the finding was unspent. /forum/ cannot host it — that page's copy is
     fully spent across masthead, claim, ledger and close, and every candidate
     beat there duplicates a section a thousand pixels above it.

     The Forum SCENE, on this page, can: every string the hold needs was
     already in it. See components/scenes/ForumScene.astro. It is also the
     right place tonally — it lands at the end of the Pilots ledger, which is
     the one event-free run this page has left, and swings the gutter from
     L* 3.7 to L* 69.5 and back inside one scene. -->
<Base>
  <Hero />
  <WhyNow />
  <MethodScene />
  <Stake />
  <PilotsScene />
  <ForumScene />
  <PeopleScene />
</Base>
